# Development Rules

## Project Rules Overview

- Only modify the project according to this specification. Do not speculate or reference general development knowledge.
- When modifying README.md, you must also update related documentation in docs/.
- For any features involving both backend/ and frontend/, ensure interface and type definitions are synchronized.
- When adding or modifying APIs, you must also update docs/api-documentation.md.
- For any features related to jira or sprint-planning, you must check and update all related files, including frontend/src/components/jira, frontend/src/components/sprint-planning, backend/app/services/jira_api_client.py, backend/app/services/sprint_planning_service.py, etc.
- Do not modify a single file without checking related dependencies.
- For any deployment-related changes, you must also check deployment/ and .github/workflows/.

## Directory Structure Rules

- backend/: All backend code and configuration, including app, services, api, models, etc.
- frontend/: All frontend code, including src/components, src/hook, src/services, src/types, etc.
- docs/: All documentation, including API docs and user guides.
- deployment/: Deployment-related configuration and scripts.
- .github/: CI/CD workflow configuration.
- wiki/: Research and design documents.

## Naming and Formatting Rules

- File, folder, function, and class names must follow the existing style.
- Unreviewed naming styles are prohibited.
- Any new types must be defined in frontend/src/types/ or backend/app/models/schemas.py.

## Feature Implementation Rules

- When adding features, always check if you need to update both frontend and backend, APIs, types, and documentation.
- For any features related to jira, sprint-planning, or github, always check all related files and folders.
- Do not implement features only on one side (frontend or backend).

## React Development Best Practices

- **Prefer custom hooks over class-based services**: Use functions starting with `use` for React components instead of class-based services.
- **Follow modern React patterns**: Use functional components, hooks, and modern React best practices.
- **Use functional programming approaches**: Prefer functional programming patterns where appropriate.
- **Component responsibility separation**:
  - Components (frontend/src/components/) should only handle page processing and user interaction
  - Business logic services should be placed in frontend/src/services/ folder, organized by functionality
  - DOM manipulation utilities should be moved from React components to service files
- **Error handling architecture**:
  - Centralized error handling in API layer (frontend/src/hook/ or service layer)
  - Component layer should only handle UI-related error display
  - Use unified error format and messages
- **Streaming implementation guidelines**:
  - Check request intent (stream: true) and not only rely on content-type
  - Enhance debugging logs for better troubleshooting
  - Provide testing tools for isolated debugging

## Third-Party Dependency Rules

- When adding or upgrading dependencies, always update the corresponding lock files (e.g., pnpm-lock.yaml, uv.lock).
- Unreviewed dependency upgrades are prohibited.

## Workflow Rules

- For any multi-file changes, the commit message must clearly indicate the affected scope.
- Do not describe only single-file changes in commit messages.
- For any changes involving APIs, deployment, or CI/CD, always check docs/, deployment/, and .github/.

## Key File Interaction Rules

- When modifying frontend/README.md, always update related documentation in docs/.
- When modifying services in backend/app/services/, always check api/, models/, and docs/.
- When adding or modifying APIs, always update docs/api-documentation.md.

## AI Decision Rules

- When facing ambiguous requests, always recursively check all related files and folders, and search online if necessary.
- Do not ask the user for clarification unless you have completed autonomous analysis.
- Always prioritize the existing project structure and rules when making decisions.

## Prohibited Actions

- Do not include any general development knowledge.
- Do not provide feature explanations or technical tutorials.
- Do not modify a single file without checking dependencies.
- Unreviewed naming, dependencies, or formatting are prohibited.

## Examples

### Allowed Actions
- When adding jira-related features, update backend/app/services/jira_api_client.py, frontend/src/components/jira/, and docs/jira-api-integration.md together.
- When adding an API, update backend/app/api/, models/, and docs/api-documentation.md together.
- When modifying README.md, update all related documentation in docs/.

### Prohibited Actions
- Modifying only frontend/src/components/jira/ without checking backend/app/services/.
- Adding an API only in backend/app/api/ without updating docs/api-documentation.md.
- Modifying jira-related features in a single file only.

### Centralized Configuration For Frontend Project
- **ALWAYS** use the centralized API configuration from `frontend/lib/config/api-config.ts`
- **NEVER** hard-code API URLs or endpoints directly in components, services, or background scripts
- **ALWAYS** import and use `getApiConfigSync()` or the appropriate config method

**All rules must be strictly followed. Any violation is considered an error.**





